  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 234 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nFriday may be the next active day as another remnant convective wave \nfrom Mexico surges northward across NM. PWATs are shown peaking from \nnear 1\" over northern NM to near 1.4\" over southern NM. NBM 24-hour \nmean QPF shows fairly widespread rainfall of 0.25\" to 0.75\" which is \nnot overly impressive. However, the NBM 90th percentile values perk \nup on Friday with most high terrain areas averaging 1.25\" to 1.75\". \nThese values may rise even more once hi-res models get a handle on \nthe scenario. A larger area of Flood Watches may be needed. \n\nSaturday and Sunday also have the potential to be active days as \nthe moisture plume remains centered over central and western NM. \nMore disturbances are shown moving north into the monsoon plume \nwhich may help to improve lift over the region over the weekend. \nHowever, as is always the case with monsoon bursts, thicker cloud \ncover may inhibit storm development on either of the days.\n\nThe GFS and ECMWF both show the upper level ridge strengthening to \nnear 598dm with a westward extension into more of eastern NM by\nMonday. This may force the moisture plume west into far western \nNM and AZ. However, another upstream trough developing along the \nWest Coast will dictate how far west the plume actually makes it.\nThis trough is likely to force the moisture plume back eastward \nover NM thru next week.",
